    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Sikhism began with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guru-nanak">Guru Nanak</a>’s teaching in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> around 1500: one God, remembered in prayer, served through honest work and sharing, and found by all people equally, whatever their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a>.</li><li>Nine Gurus after him built its institutions: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-temple">Golden Temple</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amritsar">Amritsar</a>, the scripture of the Guru Granth Sahib and, under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guru-gobind-singh">Guru Gobind Singh</a> in 1699, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalsa">Khalsa</a> order of initiated Sikhs.</li><li>Persecuted under the later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a>, Sikhs built their own empire under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranjit-singh">Ranjit Singh</a>, and today some 25 to 30 million <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhs">Sikhs</a> live in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> and across the world.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The free kitchen (langar) of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-temple">Golden Temple</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amritsar">Amritsar</a> serves meals to tens of thousands of people every day, of any faith or none, all of them seated together on the floor as equals.</p></aside>
    </section>

