    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/golden-temple"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/golden-temple.jpg" alt="Golden Temple" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/golden-temple">Golden Temple</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Golden_Temple_of_Amrithsar_7.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Shagil Kannur, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Sikhism takes its name from the Punjabi word sikh, “disciple” or “learner”: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhs">Sikhs</a> are disciples of the Guru. Its founder, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guru-nanak">Guru Nanak</a>, was born in 1469 into a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> family of the Khatri <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a> at Talwandi, now Nankana Sahib in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a>, a land where Hindu and Muslim traditions met. He worked as a storekeeper at Sultanpur Lodhi, where, according to the traditional accounts of his life, around 1499 he disappeared while bathing in the river Bein and returned three days later with the words “There is no Hindu, there is no Muslim”, proclaiming that God is beyond all religious divisions.</p><p>Nanak spent much of the following years travelling, according to tradition as far as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a>, accompanied by the Muslim musician Mardana, singing hymns of praise to the one God. He criticised empty ritual, the authority of priests and the caste system, and taught that liberation comes through devotion and the grace of God, open to all. In his later years he settled at Kartarpur on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ravi">Ravi</a> river, where a community of followers lived by his teaching, farming, praying and eating together.</p><p>Before his death in 1539 Nanak chose as his successor not one of his sons but a disciple, Angad, setting the pattern of a line of ten human Gurus. Guru Angad developed the Gurmukhi script in which the Sikh scriptures are written. Guru Amar Das organised the community into districts and made the communal kitchen, the langar, a requirement for all who came to see him. Guru Ram Das founded the town that became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amritsar">Amritsar</a> around a sacred pool in 1577.</p><p>The fifth Guru, Arjan, built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-temple">Harmandir Sahib</a>, later known as the Golden Temple, in the middle of the pool, and in 1604 compiled the Adi Granth, the first Sikh scripture, gathering the hymns of the Gurus and of Hindu and Muslim saints. The growing community attracted the suspicion of the Mughal authorities, and in 1606 Guru Arjan was executed under the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jahangir">Jahangir</a>, becoming the first Sikh martyr.</p><p>His son, Guru Hargobind, wore two swords, symbols of spiritual and temporal authority, and gave the community the means to defend itself, a turning point that shaped Sikh identity in the century that followed.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="beliefs">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Beliefs &amp; Practice</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>At the heart of Sikhism is belief in one God, the creator, without form, beyond birth and death, present in all creation. The scripture opens with the words “Ik Onkar”, “there is one God”, the beginning of the Mul Mantar, the Sikh statement of faith. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhs">Sikhs</a> most often call God Waheguru, “wonderful Lord”.</p><p>Human beings, in Sikh teaching, are separated from God by haumai, self-centredness, which binds them to the cycle of rebirth through the five vices of lust, anger, greed, attachment and pride. Release, mukti, comes through God’s grace and through nam simran, the loving remembrance of God’s name in meditation and hymn singing. The ideal is the gurmukh, the person oriented towards the Guru, as opposed to the manmukh, who follows only their own will.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guru-nanak">Guru Nanak</a>’s teaching is often summed up in three duties: to remember God (nam japna), to earn an honest living (kirat karni) and to share with others (vand chakna). Sikhism rejects renunciation of the world: the ideal is the householder who lives in the world, works, marries and serves others. It teaches the equality of all people regardless of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a>, religion or sex; service to others, seva, is a central religious act.</p><p>The Sikh Gurus rejected idol worship, pilgrimage as a means of merit, fasting and ritual purity. Worship centres on the reading and singing of the scripture. Sikhs believe that the light of the one Guru passed through all ten human Gurus, and that since 1708 the Guru has been present in the scripture, the Guru Granth Sahib, and in the community of initiated Sikhs, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalsa">Khalsa</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panth">Panth</a>.</p><p>Initiated Sikhs of the Khalsa keep the five Ks: kesh, uncut hair, usually covered by a turban; kangha, a wooden comb; kara, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a> bracelet; kachera, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> undershorts; and kirpan, a sword or dagger, a symbol of the duty to defend the weak. Men take the name Singh, “lion”, and women Kaur, “princess”.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="texts">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Texts &amp; Teachers</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/guru-gobind-singh"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/guru-gobind-singh.jpg" alt="Guru Gobind Singh" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/guru-gobind-singh">Guru Gobind Singh</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Painting_of_Guru_Gobind_Singh,_found_within_the_Anandpuri_Hazuri_Bir_codex_of_the_Dasam_Granth,_ca.1690%27s%E2%80%931700%27s_(detail).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Guru Granth Sahib is the central scripture of Sikhism and is treated as a living Guru. It was first compiled by Guru Arjan in 1604 as the Adi Granth, the “first book”, from the hymns of the first five Gurus and of saints of other traditions, among them the weaver-poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabir">Kabir</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> Shaikh Farid, the leatherworker <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ravidas">Ravidas</a> and the tailor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/namdev">Namdev</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guru-gobind-singh">Guru Gobind Singh</a> added the hymns of his father, Guru Tegh Bahadur, and before his death in 1708 declared that the Granth would be the Guru after him.</p><p>Its standard printed edition has 1,430 pages, and its hymns are arranged not by author but by the musical ragas in which they are sung. They are written in the Gurmukhi script, in a language that blends Punjabi with other north Indian languages and vocabulary from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a>, Persian and Arabic. The text is poetry meant for singing: kirtan, the singing of its hymns with music, is the heart of Sikh worship.</p><p>In the gurdwara the Guru Granth Sahib is placed on a raised platform under a canopy, attended by a reader who waves a whisk over it as a sign of honour; it is opened each morning, read at random for the day’s guidance, and laid to rest at night. A continuous reading of the whole scripture, the akhand path, takes about forty-eight hours and marks weddings, funerals and festivals.</p><p>A second collection, the Dasam Granth, contains writings attributed to Guru Gobind Singh, though the authorship of parts of it is debated. Other texts include the janamsakhis, collections of stories about the life of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guru-nanak">Guru Nanak</a> written from the 16th and 17th centuries, and the rahit-namas, manuals of conduct for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalsa">Khalsa</a>. The Sikh Rahit Maryada, issued in the 20th century, codifies the community’s code of conduct.</p><p>The Gurus’ insistence on a scripture in the language of the people, in a script of their own, gave Punjabi a literary standing and made literacy part of Sikh religious life.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="spread">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Spread</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ranjit-singh"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ranjit-singh.jpg" alt="Ranjit Singh" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ranjit-singh">Ranjit Singh</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Maharaj_Ranjit_Singh.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Colonel James Skinner (1778-1841), Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>For its first two centuries Sikhism grew among the peasants, traders and artisans of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a>, especially among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jats">Jats</a>, a large farming community. Its growth, and its development of a distinct identity and armed defence, brought conflict with the Mughal authorities. The ninth Guru, Tegh Bahadur, was executed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> in 1675 on the orders of the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a>; Sikh tradition remembers him as giving his life to defend the religious freedom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindus</a> being forced to convert.</p><p>His son, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guru-gobind-singh">Guru Gobind Singh</a>, founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalsa">Khalsa</a> on the festival of Vaisakhi in 1699 at Anandpur, initiating the first five members, the Panj Pyare or “five beloved ones”, with sweetened water stirred with a sword, and then being initiated by them in turn. The Khalsa gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhs">Sikhs</a> a disciplined community of soldier-saints. Gobind Singh fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a> and the hill chiefs, lost all four of his sons and died in 1708.</p><p>After his death <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banda-singh-bahadur">Banda Singh Bahadur</a> led a peasant rebellion that briefly ruled parts of the Punjab before he was captured and executed in Delhi in 1716. Through the 18th century Sikhs endured persecution and the invasions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nader-shah">Nader Shah</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-shah-abdali">Ahmad Shah Durrani</a>, who blew up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-temple">Harmandir Sahib</a> in 1762, and organised themselves into confederacies, the misls, which gradually took control of the Punjab as Mughal power collapsed.</p><p>In 1799 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranjit-singh">Ranjit Singh</a>, leader of one of the misls, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a> and built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikh-empire">Sikh Empire</a>, which ruled the Punjab, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a> and the north-west frontier until after his death. He covered the upper part of the Harmandir Sahib in gilded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a>, giving it the name of the Golden Temple. Defeated in two wars with the British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a>, the Sikh state was annexed in 1849.</p><p>Under British rule Sikhs were recruited in large numbers into the army and emigrated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malaya">Malaya</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-kong">Hong Kong</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-africa">East Africa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>. The partition of the Punjab in 1947 divided the Sikh homeland and forced most Sikhs in the west to flee to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Migration since then has created large communities in Britain, Canada and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="community">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Community &amp; Society</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/akal-takht"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/akal-takht.jpg" alt="Akal Takht" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/akal-takht">Akal Takht</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:AkalTakhtGoldenTempleComplex.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Paasikivi, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The centre of Sikh community life is the gurdwara, “the door of the Guru”, where the scripture is installed. Anyone may enter, regardless of religion, provided they cover their head and remove their shoes. Worship consists of hymn singing, readings and the ardas, the communal prayer, followed by karah prashad, a sweet offering shared by all.</p><p>Every gurdwara has a langar, a free communal kitchen, introduced by the early Gurus to break down the barriers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a>: everyone sits together on the floor and eats the same simple vegetarian meal, prepared and served by volunteers. The langar of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-temple">Golden Temple</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amritsar">Amritsar</a> serves tens of thousands of people every day.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalsa">Khalsa</a>, the order of initiated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhs">Sikhs</a>, takes its members through the amrit ceremony, in which they vow to keep the five Ks and the code of conduct and to abstain from tobacco and intoxicants. Many Sikhs are not formally initiated but keep some or all of its outward signs; the turban has become a distinctive symbol of Sikh identity around the world.</p><p>Sikhism has no priesthood. Authority rests with the scripture and with the community gathered in its presence. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akal-takht">Akal Takht</a>, the “throne of the timeless one” facing the Golden Temple, founded by Guru Hargobind, is the highest seat of temporal authority, and since the 1920s the Shiromani Gurdwara Parbandhak Committee, an elected body formed during a movement to free the gurdwaras from hereditary custodians, has managed the main shrines of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a>.</p><p>Equality between men and women is a principle of the Gurus’ teaching: women may lead prayers and read the scripture. In practice caste distinctions have persisted in Sikh society, a tension that reformers within the community have long addressed.</p><p>Modern Sikh history has also known great suffering. In June 1984 the Indian army stormed the Golden Temple, where armed militants led by Jarnail Singh Bhindranwale had fortified themselves; many were killed, including pilgrims. In October Prime Minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indira-gandhi">Indira Gandhi</a> was assassinated by two of her Sikh bodyguards, and thousands of Sikhs were killed in the violence that followed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> and other cities.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Sikhism is one of the youngest of the world’s major religions and, with some 25 to 30 million followers, among the largest. Most <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhs">Sikhs</a> live in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a>, where they form the majority of the population of the Indian state, and the diaspora has made the turban and the gurdwara familiar in cities from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toronto">Toronto</a> to Vancouver and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>.</p><p>Its teaching of one God, open to all, its insistence on honest work and sharing, and its rejection of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a> and ritual made it a powerful reforming movement in the religious history of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. The langar, which feeds anyone who comes, has become a model of community service; Sikh organisations run free kitchens in times of disaster around the world.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-temple">Golden Temple</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amritsar">Amritsar</a>, with its gilded sanctuary reflected in the sacred pool, is one of the most visited places of worship on earth. Sikh devotional music, the kirtan, is a distinctive tradition of Indian music, and the Guru Granth Sahib is among the great collections of devotional poetry of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-asia">South Asia</a>.</p><p>Sikh soldiers served in large numbers in the British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-army">Indian Army</a> in both world wars, and Sikhs have played a prominent part in the politics, agriculture and economy of modern India. Their history of persecution, martyrdom and resistance, from the execution of Guru Arjan to the violence of 1984, remains central to Sikh memory and identity.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Sikhism, founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guru-nanak">Guru Nanak</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> around 1500 and shaped by nine Gurus after him, teaches devotion to one God, honest work, sharing and the equality of all people. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalsa">Khalsa</a> founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guru-gobind-singh">Guru Gobind Singh</a> in 1699 and the Guru Granth Sahib, its living scripture, define its community, which today numbers some 25 to 30 million people in the Punjab and around the world.</em></p>

